Title: The Velveteen Rabbit  
Credit: Written by  
Author: Author  
Source: Adapted from book  
Draft date: 7/30/2026

FADE IN:

INT. NURSERY - CHRISTMAS MORNING

A cozy, warmly lit nursery. In a grand stocking hung by the fireplace, a plump, velveteen rabbit sits among nuts, oranges, a toy engine, and other treasures. A sprig of holly peeks from his paws, his spotted brown and white coat and pink-lined ears exuding charm. The camera lingers on his soft, thread-whiskered face as the excitement of Christmas fills the room.

  
INT. NURSERY - NEAR THE FENDER - DAY

The Velveteen Rabbit lies beside the aged, patchy Skin Horse. Dust motes swirl in the gentle light as they relax near the fender.

SKIN HORSE

Real isn't how you are made. It's a thing that happens to you. When a child loves you for a long, long time, not just to play with, but REALLY loves you, then you become Real.

  
RABBIT

Does it hurt?

  
SKIN HORSE

Sometimes. When you are Real you don't mind being hurt.

  
RABBIT

Does it happen all at once, like being wound up... or bit by bit?

  
SKIN HORSE

It doesn't happen all at once. You become. It takes a long time.

A tender pause passes between them as the nursery magic seems to hum in the silence.

  
INT. BOY'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

The nursery is dimly lit by a night-light on the mantel. The Velveteen Rabbit is gently placed in the Boy's arms after Nana’s hurried declaration.

NANA

Here, take your old Bunny! He'll do to sleep with you!

  
The Boy, small and earnest, cradles the Rabbit close. Later, as the Boy drifts to sleep, his murmurs in the dark are soft yet determined.

BOY

(whispering, half-asleep)
You mustn't say that. You aren't just a toy. You're… Real.

  
The Rabbit nestles under the warm chin of the Boy, a quiet satisfaction glowing in his eyes.

  
EXT. GARDEN - SPRING TIME

Sunlight spills over a blossoming garden where the Boy and the Rabbit share adventures. The Boy pushes the Rabbit gently in a wheelbarrow and constructs tiny tunnels beneath bedclothes fashioned into burrows.

Nearby, a pair of sleek, new wild rabbits appear. Their fur is shiny and seamless.

  
WILD RABBIT

Why don't you get up and play with us?

  
RABBIT

I don't feel like it...

  
WILD RABBIT

(stepping close, inspecting)
He hasn't got any hind legs!

  
RABBIT

(offended)
I have! I have got hind legs! I am sitting on them!

  
WILD RABBIT

Then stretch them out and show me, like this!

The wild rabbit begins to whirl and dance exuberantly. The Velveteen Rabbit hesitates, longing to join but too shy, his insecurities evident in his stillness.

  
EXT. BOY'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

In a quiet room, a feverish Boy tosses in his sleep. His flushed face and labored breathing reveal illness. The Velveteen Rabbit lies by his side, his fur now a little worn, yet he remains ever faithful.

  
NANA (O.S.)

How about his old Bunny?

  
DOCTOR (O.S.)

(getting involved, matter-of-fact)
That? It's a mass of scarlet fever germs! Burn it at once. Nonsense—get him a new one. He mustn't have that any more!

A somber mood fills the room as the Rabbit's fate seems sealed by a decision beyond his control.

  
EXT. GARDEN BEHIND THE FOWL-HOUSE - ANXIOUS TIMES

Under a fading light, the Velveteen Rabbit finds himself in a sack alongside old picture-books and discarded toys. The sack lies untied; the Rabbit peeks through the opening. His once-vibrant fur is now shabby and his form is worn by time and love.

He watches the raspberry thicket in the distance, memories of sunlit picnics and gentle garden games flooding back. A single, genuine tear slips from his little velveteen nose and lands on the cold soil.

  
EXT. GARDEN BEHIND THE FOWL-HOUSE - NIGHT

Where the tear falls, the earth stirs. A mysterious flower with slender emerald leaves and a blossom like a golden cup begins to grow. Slowly, the flower opens to reveal a luminous figure.

  
FAIRY

(softly, with wonder)
Little Rabbit, don't you know who I am?

  
RABBIT

(voice trembling)
I... I was Real to the Boy. Wasn't I Real before?

  
FAIRY

I am the nursery magic Fairy. I take care of the playthings that have been loved. When they are old and worn, and the children no longer need them, I come and turn them into Real. You were Real to the Boy... now you shall be Real to everyone.

She gathers the trembling Rabbit into her arms, pressing a tender kiss to his damp, velveteen nose.

  
EXT. GARDEN - NIGHT

The Fairy lifts the Rabbit and flies over the dewy garden. In an instant, something marvelous occurs. The Rabbit’s faded fur transforms into soft, shiny brown; his form becomes lithe, and suddenly—in a moment of pure magic—he discovers that he now has hind legs.

  
EXT. WOOD - NIGHT

In a moonlit clearing among towering trees, wild rabbits begin a joyful dance upon the velvet grass. The newly transformed Rabbit hesitates at first, then, with a burst of exuberance, leaps and twirls on his hind legs. His eyes sparkle with the joy of being truly alive.

  
EXT. GARDEN - SPRING DAY

Later, in a sunny garden, the Boy plays happily among the flowers. He pauses, noticing a rabbit with familiar soft eyes and gentle markings approaching quietly.

  
BOY

(astonished, joyful)
Why, he looks just like my old Bunny that was lost when I had scarlet fever!

The rabbit, now full of life and radiance, sits contentedly as the Boy smiles in recognition and love.

  
FADE OUT.
